\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Cycle decimation sends laws to laws}
\date{2026-11-06}

\p{Let #{q\ge 2} and #{N\ge 3} be integers, and let #{Q} be a
\vocabk{uniform cycle precision}{fdip-00AW} on
#{\mathbb Z/(qN)\mathbb Z} with neighbor value #{b>0} and shape
#{t}. Let #{S} and #{C} be the retained precision and the
eliminated operator compression supplied by \ref{fdip-00B3}.
The retained coupling
is #{b/U_{q-1}(t)} and the retained shape is #{T_q(t)}.
Then #{Q} is positive definite if and only if #{t>1}. In
that case the retained coupling is positive and the retained
shape is greater than #{1}. The coupling-flow Remark of
\ref{fdip-00B3} records this step.
The
\vocabk{Gaussian partition function}{fdip-0005} factors as
##{Z_Q=(2\pi)^{N(q-1)/2}(\det C)^{-1/2}Z_S,}
and the \vocabk{Gaussian induced action}{fdip-005R} adds
#{(N/2)\log(b^{q-1}U_{q-1}(t))}, where #{U_{q-1}} is the
polynomial of \ref{fdip-00B1}. At #{t=1} the
constant fields are a zero mode, so there is no Gaussian law on
all field values. At #{t=-1/2}, the other fixed point of #{T_2}
over an integral domain in which #{2} is invertible, as in
\ref{fdip-00B1}, a negative eigenvalue occurs. When
#{t>1}, for every integer #{r\ge 2}, the induced-action shifts
of a step by #{r} and a following step by #{q} add to the shift
of the single step by #{qr}.}

\proof{
\p{On a cycle of length #{n}, and for each integer #{k}, the
mode with value #{\omega^{jk}} at vertex #{j}, where
#{\omega=\ue^{2\pi \ui /n}}, receives #{-b} from each neighbor.
The value at a neighbor differs by the factor #{\omega^{\pm k}},
so those couplings contribute
#{-b(\omega^{k}+\omega^{-k})=-2b\cos(2\pi k/n)}. The
eigenvalue is
#{a-2b\cos(2\pi k/n)=2b\bigl(t-\cos(2\pi k/n)\bigr)}.
The constant mode #{k=0} has eigenvalue #{2b(t-1)}. Since
#{\cos\le 1}, this is the smallest eigenvalue. For #{b>0} it
is positive if and only if #{t>1}, and then every eigenvalue
is positive. At #{t=1} the constant eigenvalue vanishes. At
#{t=-1/2} it equals #{-3b}, which is negative.
\ref{fdip-00B1} says that, in an integral domain in which #{2}
is invertible, #{t=-1/2} is the other fixed point of
#{T_2}, and that
#{U_{q-1}(\cosh\theta)=\sinh(q\theta)/\sinh\theta>0} whenever
#{t=\cosh\theta>1}. Thus the retained shape is
#{T_q(t)=\cosh(q\theta)>1}. Since #{b>0}, the retained
coupling #{b/U_{q-1}(t)} of \ref{fdip-00B3} is positive.
The same evaluation is the coupling-flow Remark of
\ref{fdip-00B3}: the step sends #{\theta} to #{q\theta}.}

\p{The counting inner product makes the kept and eliminated
coordinates orthogonal. For #{t>1} the precision #{Q} is
positive, so \ref{fdip-000D} supplies the scalar
#{(2\pi)^{N(q-1)/2}(\det C)^{-1/2}} and the identity
#{\det Q=\det C\det S}. \ref{fdip-000E} identifies the
pushforward with the Gaussian law of #{S}. The induced action
adds half the logarithm of #{\det C}. The determinant formula
of \ref{fdip-00B3} makes that logarithm
#{(N/2)\log(b^{q-1}U_{q-1}(t))}, and the argument is positive.
\ref{fdip-00B1} gives #{U_k(\cosh\theta)>0} for every integer
#{k\ge 0} when #{t=\cosh\theta>1}. The composite step in
\ref{fdip-00B3} therefore applies for every integer #{r\ge 2}.
Its eliminated determinants multiply, and the induced-action
shifts add.}
}
